    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**Yes, this is an exceptional offer.** It sits firmly in the **top decile (85th\u201390th+ percentile)** for a Senior Product Manager (L6) role at Google in the Bay Area. \n\nFor context:\n* **Base Salary:** At **$260,000**, you are right at the ceiling of the published band ($183,000 \u2013 $271,000).\n* **Initial Equity ($1.34M):** Standard L6 equity grants typically fall between $600,000 and $900,000 over 4 years. An initial grant of $1.34M is comparable to what candidates with multiple competing Tier-1 offers receive.\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($200,000):** Most L6 sign-on bonuses range from $50,000 to $100,000. A $200,000 sign-on fully protects you against any Meta unvested cliff ($200k over the next 12 months).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Year-by-Year Financial Breakdown\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial new-hire equity: **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** across four years.\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $260,000 | $260,000+ | $260,000+ | $260,000+ |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 |\n| **RSUs (GSUs)** | $442,200 *(33%)* | $442,200 *(33%)* | $294,800 *(22%)* | $160,800 *(12%)* |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $200,000 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| **Estimated Total Comp (TC)** | **~$954,200** | **~$754,200** | **~$606,800** | **~$472,800** |\n\n* **4-Year Average Annual TC (Straight-line):** **~$697,000/year** (vs. your current **$526,000/year** at Meta).\n* **The \"Year 3/4 Dip\" & Refresher Grants:** Google\u2019s annual refresher grants stack over time. Assuming standard performance, annual refreshes granted at the end of Years 1, 2, and 3 will gradually backfill the step-down in Years 3 and 4.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** Absolutely not. The role is on your top-choice team, and the comp represents a material step up in both cash flow and career upside.\n* **Accept As-Is vs. Negotiate?** \n  Because you have **no competing offers**, aggressive negotiation carries little leverage. However, you can make a single, low-friction, high-confidence ask using a **\"one-and-done closing anchor\"** (i.e., *\"If you can do X, I will sign immediately\"*). \n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For and How to Frame It\n\nBecause your base is already near the band ceiling ($271k) and your sign-on is already very high, **equity** is the easiest lever for the compensation committee, followed by a slight base adjustment.\n\n#### The Ask:\n* **Equity:** Request a bump from **$1.34M \u2192 $1.45M \u2013 $1.5M** over 4 years (an extra ~$25k\u2013$40k/year in front-loaded stock).\n* **Alternative (Base):** Request moving base to the top of band at **$270,000**.\n\n#### Negotiation Script (Email / Phone):\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this together. I\u2019m genuinely excited about the team, the scope of the role, and the impact I can have here. Google remains my clear first choice.*\n>\n> *I want to be fully transparent: I am walking away from strong momentum and unvested equity at Meta, but I am ready to commit to Google. If we can get the equity grant to **$1.45M** (or bring base to **$270,000**), I will sign the offer letter today and immediately start the transition process.*\n>\n> *Either way, I really appreciate your partnership through this process and look forward to finalizing things.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Due Diligence Questions Before Signing\n1. **Sign-on Terms:** Confirm whether the $200,000 sign-on is paid lump-sum on your first paycheck and verify the clawback duration (typically 12 or 24 months prorated).\n2. **Vesting Cadence:** Verify that your GSUs vest **monthly** (standard for L6 at Google) after your initial start period.",
